  "text": "Lexar NM790 with Heatsink 2TB PCIe Gen4 M.2 NVMe SSD Review Facebook Flipboard Instagram X Youtube Sign in Home Forums News Tech Gaming Culture Security Press Releases CES Computex Reviews CPU AMD Intel Video Cards AMD RX 9000 Series AMD RX 9070 Series AMD RX 9060 Series AMD RX 7000 Series AMD RX 7900 Series AMD RX 7800 Series AMD RX 7700 Series AMD RX 7600 Series AMD RX 6000 Series AMD RX 5000 Series NVIDIA RTX 5000 Series NVIDIA RTX 5090 Series NVIDIA RTX 5080 Series NVIDIA RTX 5070 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 5070 Series NVIDIA RTX 5060 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 5060 Series NVIDIA RTX 5050 Series NVIDIA RTX 4000 Series NVIDIA RTX 4090 Series NVIDIA RTX 4080 Series NVIDIA RTX 4070 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 4070 Series NVIDIA RTX 4060 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 4060 Series NVIDIA RTX 3000 Series NVIDIA RTX 3090 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3090 Series NVIDIA RTX 3080 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3080 Series NVIDIA RTX 3070 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3070 Series NVIDIA RTX 3060 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3060 Series NVIDIA RTX 3050 Series NVIDIA RTX 2000 Series NVIDIA GTX 1600 Series Intel Arc Series Power Supplies Motherboards Storage Cooling Cases Memory Game Performance Reviews Gaming Chairs Quick Compare Peripherals Keyboards Mice Overclocking Video Cards Specifications CPU Specifications GPU Family Specifications Video Card Specifications Motherboard Specifications NVMe SSD Specifications Deals Computex CES Editorial Build Guides High End Enthusiast Mid-Range Budget YouTube Channel YouTube Channel FPS Reviewcast Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Privacy Policy Password recovery Recover your password your email Search In Your Base, Reviewing Your FPSes Support Us on Patreon Facebook Flipboard Instagram Patreon X Youtube Search Home Forums News Tech Gaming Culture Security Press Releases CES Computex Reviews CPU AMD Intel Video Cards AMD RX 9000 Series AMD RX 9070 Series AMD RX 9060 Series AMD RX 7000 Series AMD RX 7900 Series AMD RX 7800 Series AMD RX 7700 Series AMD RX 7600 Series AMD RX 6000 Series AMD RX 5000 Series NVIDIA RTX 5000 Series NVIDIA RTX 5090 Series NVIDIA RTX 5080 Series NVIDIA RTX 5070 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 5070 Series NVIDIA RTX 5060 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 5060 Series NVIDIA RTX 5050 Series NVIDIA RTX 4000 Series NVIDIA RTX 4090 Series NVIDIA RTX 4080 Series NVIDIA RTX 4070 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 4070 Series NVIDIA RTX 4060 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 4060 Series NVIDIA RTX 3000 Series NVIDIA RTX 3090 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3090 Series NVIDIA RTX 3080 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3080 Series NVIDIA RTX 3070 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3070 Series NVIDIA RTX 3060 Ti Series NVIDIA RTX 3060 Series NVIDIA RTX 3050 Series NVIDIA RTX 2000 Series NVIDIA GTX 1600 Series Intel Arc Series Power Supplies Motherboards Storage Cooling Cases Memory Game Performance Reviews Gaming Chairs Quick Compare Peripherals Keyboards Mice Overclocking Video Cards Specifications CPU Specifications GPU Family Specifications Video Card Specifications Motherboard Specifications NVMe SSD Specifications Deals Computex CES Editorial Build Guides High End Enthusiast Mid-Range Budget YouTube Channel YouTube Channel FPS Reviewcast Lexar NM790 with Heatsink 2TB PCIe Gen4 M.2 NVMe SSD Review Reviews Storage By Brent Justice April 17, 2024 Share Facebook X ReddIt Email Copy URL 9 OVERALL SCORE The FPS Review may receive a commission if you purchase something after clicking a link in this article. Table of Contents: Page 1: Introduction Page 2: Test Setup Page 3: System Storage Benchmarks Page 4: Throughput Benchmarks (SEQ and RND) Page 5: Game Load Time Performance Page 6: File Copy and Workstation Performance Page 7: Conclusion Page 7: Final Points Introduction Lexar is well known for its memory components, including memory cards, USB flash drives, portable drives, card readers, RAM, and SSDs.  We’ve evaluated two Lexar SSDs, the Lexar Professional NM800 Pro SSD is geared for professionals, and professional applications and we’ve also evaluated the Lexar NM710 SSD which is a more value-conscious SSD, but both are PCIe Gen4 x4 NVMe SSDs. Another option, which we are reviewing today, is the Lexar NM790 SSD which fits in above the NM710 but offers competitive general use and gaming-oriented performance in a DRAM-less configuration, but at the highest generation PCIe Gen4 NVMe SSD speeds.  The Lexar NM790 offers 7,400MB/s read and 6,500MB/s write performance and uses Host Memory Buffer (HMB) 3.0 instead of a dedicated DRAM cache, saving cost and reducing energy consumption and does have Dynamic SLC caching. Lexar offers the NM790 SSD in two variations, one without a heatsink and one with an integrated pre-installed heatsink.  We are specifically reviewing the NM790 with Heatsink M.2 2280 PCIe Gen 4×4 NVMe SSD part number LNM790X002T-RN9NU.  Lexar offers the NM790 in three capacities, 1TB, 2TB, and 4TB, so it is great to see a 4TB model, meaning you can get high-speed performance and large capacity for a laptop or desktop or the PS5. Lexar NM790 PCIe Generation Gen4 x4 Capacities Offered 1TB, 2TB, 4TB Versions No Heatsink, With Heatsink Cell Type TLC Controller Maxio MAP1602 NAND Flash 232-Layer DRAM Cache DRAM-less, Host Memory Buffer 3.0 SLC Caching Yes Read Speed 7400MB/s Write Speed 6500MB/s TBW 1TB: 1000TBW, 2TB: 1500TBW, 4TB: 3000TBW Quick Facts at a Glance What’s also great about all of these capacities is that all three offer the exact same read and write performance: Sequential read up to 7400MB/s, and sequential write up to 6500MB/s, so no matter which capacity you purchase, you are getting the same performance.  The TBW isn’t bad either: 1TB is  1000TBW, 2TB is 1500TBW, and 4TB is 3000TBW, MTBF is 1,500,000 hours and Lexar offers a five-year limited warranty. For the Lexar NM790 with integrated Heatsink version, the 1TB is about $80 online at the time of writing, while the 2TB is $154 and the 4TB is $268.  Prices are shifting, so make sure to check our pricing widget below for the latest updated prices. Lexar 1TB NM790 SSD with Heatsink PCIe Gen4 NVMe M.2 2280 Internal Solid State Drive, Up to 7400/6500 MB/s Read/Write, Compatible with PS5, for Gamers and... $204.99 in stock Lexar 2TB NM790 SSD with Heatsink PCIe Gen4 NVMe M.2 2280 Internal Solid State Drive, Up to 7400/6500 MB/s Read/Write, Compatible with PS5, for Gamers and... $334.99 in stock Lexar 4TB NM790 SSD with Heatsink PCIe Gen4 NVMe M.2 2280 Internal Solid State Drive, Up to 7400/6500 MB/s Read/Write, Compatible with PS5, for Gamers and... $859.99 in stock Amazon price updated: November 9, 2026 11:28 am Lexar NM790 with Heatsink 2TB SSD As we mentioned, the Lexar NM790 is a PCIe Gen4 x4 NVMe 2.0 M.2 2280 SSD.  The Lexar NM790 is based on the Maxio MAP1602 controller and 232-Layer TLC 3D NAND Flash.  It does not have a DRAM cache, instead relying on Host Memory Buffer (HMB) 3.0 and a dynamic SLC cache.  The Maxio MAP1602 is a 4-channel controller based on a 12nm process technology IC that supports PCIe Gen4x4 NVMe 2.0, and Maxio Agile ECC 3 technology based on an ARM R5 CPU core. We have the NM790 with a pre-installed heatsink, and the box is eye-catching the capacity and speed are clearly labeled, and the label of the SSD shows through a window so you know what model and specs you are getting.  We found the NM790 inside to be securely held in place, shipping should be no problem. The Lexar NM790 is a standard 2280 M.2 heatsink, and Lexar has pre-installed the heatsink on this model, which covers both sides of the SSD.  However, note that all the silicon is on one side only.  Regardless, Lexar has designed this heatsink with thermal pads on both sides, which make contact with the all-aluminum heatsink to transfer heat on both sides. Upon close inspection, you can see that the heatsink has channeled fins down the center, to allow air to pass through from your normal case airflow and direct the airflow.  Even with this heatsink, Lexar has provided a slim profile so that it will fit in a laptop or the PS5 even with this heatsink on.  The cooling is more than sufficient, without active airflow for the performance this SSD delivers.  If you so desire, you can remove the heatsink, but at the point you might as well just purchase the NM790 without the heatsink if you want to use your own or have a very thin laptop.  It’s great that you have these options with the NM790. When installed, the Lexar NM790 SSD offers a great industrial blacked-out aesthetic that will match well in any system.  The slim-profile heatsink ensures excellent cooling and will not interfere with larger components in your system. Join the discussion in The FPS Review Forums... Page 1: Introduction Page 2: Test Setup Page 3: System Storage Benchmarks Page 4: Throughput Benchmarks (SEQ and RND) Page 5: Game Load Time Performance Page 6: File Copy and Workstation Performance Page 7: Conclusion Page 7: Final Points Next › REVIEW OVERVIEW Performance 9 Features 9 Cooling 10 Price Value 8 SUMMARY In our Lexar NM790 SSD review, we found that it offers top-tier Gen4 SSD performance, without a DRAM cache, but offers fast access time, great sequential read and write performance, and good random performance.  You'll find fast loading times for games, fast file copy times, and even workstation capable.  The integrated heatsink cools well, and provides stable performance without throttling.  The price is steeper than the competition, but the integrated heatsink makes up the difference. 9 OVERALL SCORE Discussion Join Discussion → No replies yet. Be the first to reply on the forum. Tags: Lexar lexar nm790 M.2 nm790 NVMe PCIe Gen4 pcie gen4 x4 PCIe Gen4x4 SSD Storage Brent Justice https://www.thefpsreview.com Former managing editor/reviewer of GPUs at HardOCP (18 years 2001-2019), & former writer for Sharky Extreme, Brent Justice has been reviewing computer components since the 90s, educated in the art and method of the computer hardware review, and gaming, he brings experience, knowledge, and hands-on testing with a gamer-oriented and hardware enthusiast perspective.  You can follow him on Twitter - [@Brent_Justice] and You can follow him on YT - [Justice Gaming] [@JusticeGamingChannel] Share Facebook X ReddIt Email Copy URL Search Recent News AMD Is Reportedly Prepping for RDNA 5 by Adding a Swath of Driver Patches for GFX13 News November 10, 2026 GPU Taskman Is a New Open-Source Monitoring App That Enables Users to See What Apps Are Using VRAM and More News November 10, 2026 Older LG WOLED Panels Have Been Spotted Experiencing “Edge Rot” Condition News November 9, 2026 The FPS Review Weekender: Pairing a New GPU With an Old CPU? A Radeon RX 9060 XT Can Beat the RTX 5070, Plus 10... News November 7, 2026 Latest VBIOS Update Is Reportedly Bricking ASRock Radeon RX 7900 XT/XTX Graphics Cards News November 6, 2026 Hub Our weekly newsletter includes a recap of our reviews and a run down of the most popular tech news that we published. Email address: Leave this field empty if you're human: © 2026 DCS Reviews, LLC. Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Forums Contact us Facebook Facebook Patreon Patreon X X Youtube Youtube",
